<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lex Baiuvariorum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Lex Baiuvariorum is the law code of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a>, written in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and compiled probably in the 740s under Frankish influence, drawing on Alemannic, Visigothic and Frankish laws. It names the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agilolfings">Agilolfings</a> as the hereditary ducal family and gives special status to five other noble kindreds. It remained in force under Carolingian rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lex-baiuvariorum.jpg" alt="Lex Baiuvariorum" width="320" height="581" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Schreiber unbekannt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prolog_lex_baioariorum_ingolstadt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
